WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.070
Welcome back to the Cybersecurity Architecture Series.

00:00:03.070 --> 00:00:11.019
In the previous two videos, I talked about identity management and endpoint security, and now we're going to focus on the network.

00:00:11.470 --> 00:00:16.050
The network security involves a lot of different elements, and we're going to talk about each of these--

00:00:16.050 --> 00:00:20.377
--about firewalls, which are a fundamental component of this, about segmentation,

00:00:20.377 --> 00:00:25.745
which we're able to do with using firewalls, about virtual private networks, about SASE.

00:00:26.110 --> 00:00:27.730
You'll hear more about that in a few minutes.

00:00:27.940 --> 00:00:32.469
And then, actually, the topic is so large and so mature, you really can't cover it all in this space.

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:35.409
So there's going to be some things I'm not going to get a chance to talk about.

00:00:35.650 --> 00:00:40.390
But needless to say, there's a lot more that could be discussed here.

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:43.719
So, first thing we're going to talk about are firewalls.

00:00:44.170 --> 00:00:46.603
So let's talk about what are we going to do with firewalls.

00:00:46.870 --> 00:00:53.170
The idea behind them in the first place where the technology and the notion even came from is in the physical world, a physical firewall.

00:00:53.410 --> 00:00:55.810
Let's say we've got three townhouses all connected.

00:00:56.290 --> 00:00:58.299
And this guy has a fire in his unit.

00:00:58.660 --> 00:01:04.929
Well, what we'd like is to have some way to limit the spread of that fire from one unit to the next.

00:01:04.930 --> 00:01:10.690
It might not make it completely go away, but it slows it down so that the fire department can get there and do something about it.

00:01:11.050 --> 00:01:16.240
So it's a way of creating isolation and protection from a dangerous event.

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:21.310
So take that concept in mind and then apply it to a network scenario.

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:27.670
Here we've got a user on a workstation hitting a web server, and that web server then goes against a database.

00:01:28.270 --> 00:01:33.709
The typical architecture we put in here is we add a firewall here and a firewall here.

00:01:33.970 --> 00:01:38.170
One that's Internet facing and one that is internal facing.

00:01:38.470 --> 00:01:43.260
And what we're going to do, the reason we're putting two of these here, we'll talk more about it in segmentation.

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:51.430
But simply what we're going to do here, in the most basic form, is we're going to filter based upon certain things that are happening here.

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:54.400
This is this notion of packet filtering.

00:01:54.820 --> 00:02:02.050
With packet filtering, I'm going to look into the packet, that is, the information that's being sent from this guy to this guy.

00:02:02.530 --> 00:02:08.890
And in that, he's going to include things like his source address, that is, the address that he's coming from, his destination address,

00:02:08.890 --> 00:02:11.672
where he wants it to go, which is going to be this web server

00:02:11.672 --> 00:02:16.389
and the port that he's going to use, which is a way of designating what kind of traffic it is.

00:02:16.930 --> 00:02:21.939
So this firewall can then filter based upon that information that's in the header of that packet.

00:02:22.450 --> 00:02:29.439
And he's going to look at and say, okay, port 80, that's the standard industry standard for unencrypted Internet traffic.

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:34.689
So I'm going to allow that web traffic to come through on port 80, that we will allow.

00:02:34.990 --> 00:02:37.539
We will also allow encrypted traffic.

00:02:37.660 --> 00:02:41.680
So this is your SSL or now TLS encrypted web traffic.

00:02:42.010 --> 00:02:47.620
So I'm going to open this first firewall to allow that information to flow through.

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:50.860
But I'm also going to add some more things to make it a little tighter.

00:02:51.580 --> 00:02:56.800
I'm going to say the source address has to be in the range of the external Internet.

00:02:57.730 --> 00:02:58.690
Why would we look at that?

00:02:58.690 --> 00:03:03.219
Well, I want to make sure that somebody isn't spoofing and acting like they're coming from inside.

00:03:03.580 --> 00:03:09.189
Sometimes we'll trust traffic that comes from the inside and give it more privileges and sort of drop our guard.

00:03:09.460 --> 00:03:11.979
We don't want that to happen from external traffic.

00:03:11.980 --> 00:03:17.530
So if this packet is literally coming from the outside and claims to be coming from the inside, we're going to block it.

00:03:17.710 --> 00:03:19.120
It's just going to be blocked right here.

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:25.210
The destination address, we're going to say, where you can go is you must in fact only go there.

00:03:25.720 --> 00:03:30.743
If this guy tries to put any other address, like here, if he tries to go to the database directly,

00:03:30.743 --> 00:03:36.009
that will be blocked, because that destination address is not something we're going to allow through the first firewall.

00:03:36.580 --> 00:03:39.879
Now we have a second firewall here that's going to add even a little more security.

00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:47.050
It's going to say this is the traffic, this is the port we're going to open up to allow this kind of traffic between the web server and the database.

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:51.430
We're going to allow a source address only of the web server.

00:03:51.700 --> 00:03:57.448
So I'm not going to allow anything that came from the Internet, the external world, if it's got that address as its source,

00:03:57.448 --> 00:03:59.710
where it started from, I'm not going to allow it.

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:05.889
It has to have originated here and its destination can only be that.

00:04:06.490 --> 00:04:14.636
So what we've done in creating that set of rules is any traffic from the outside can only get to and must stop over here

00:04:14.636 --> 00:04:18.879
where it can be inspected and where we can implement some sort of security controls.

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:20.529
It cannot get anywhere else.

00:04:20.890 --> 00:04:26.769
So that's what we're basically doing here, and you can keep applying these kinds of rules in order to tighten up your security.

00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:34.952
And what we're doing, what I've described here, is basically this idea of packet filtering where we're essentially just looking here at the packet

00:04:34.952 --> 00:04:38.679
that is, the first part of the packet, the header.

00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:40.959
So think of it as like an envelope.

00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:44.619
If I'm mailing a letter to you, what's going to be on the envelope?

00:04:44.620 --> 00:04:48.490
Well, we're going to have a to address and we're going to have a return address.

00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:50.199
This is your to address.

00:04:50.200 --> 00:04:51.489
This is your return address.

00:04:51.940 --> 00:04:53.920
So that's what we've basically done here.

00:04:54.070 --> 00:04:56.080
But we have not looked inside.

00:04:56.500 --> 00:05:00.320
So there's no inspection, here.

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:04.160
But in this next example, I'm going to talk about stateful packet inspection.

00:05:04.700 --> 00:05:08.342
Where in this case, we're going to look not only at the packet,

00:05:08.342 --> 00:05:13.819
but we may also go ahead and look at the full thing, look at what's in the payload as well.

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:19.720
And there are other things like application firewalls that are specific and they'll do even more inspection of this payload.

00:05:19.720 --> 00:05:24.680
That is the data that you're actually sending and make sure that it's not going to do harm to us.

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:31.567
And in this case, it's more like the open envelope so that we're able to see the contents,

00:05:31.567 --> 00:05:35.720
not only the to and the from, but we can also see the contents as well.

00:05:35.900 --> 00:05:38.389
So that's an analogy to help you understand what this is.

00:05:38.930 --> 00:05:42.199
Stateful packet inspection also looks at the context of the packet.

00:05:42.530 --> 00:05:49.279
So it's looking at you sent one of these, and then one of these, but you should never have sent that next thing because that breaks all the rules.

00:05:49.580 --> 00:05:55.009
So it can look at more than just individual packets in isolation, which is what packet filtering does.

00:05:55.310 --> 00:06:02.149
This was sort of our first generation of firewalling, and then it got more sophisticated when we added stateful packet inspection.

00:06:02.690 --> 00:06:07.160
But there are other things here as well in this sort of firewall and technologies.

00:06:07.160 --> 00:06:12.709
Think about these all as a collection of technologies that can be part or not of a particular firewall.

00:06:13.010 --> 00:06:15.470
And the next one is this idea of a proxy.

00:06:16.010 --> 00:06:20.420
A proxy is something that acts on behalf of something else.

00:06:20.870 --> 00:06:28.788
So we have here a workstation that's coming in from the outside and it wants to hit this web server, for instance.

00:06:29.510 --> 00:06:35.660
Except what I'm going to do is say I'm going to put another server here in between that is the proxy.

00:06:36.170 --> 00:06:38.240
And what's going to happen is I'm going to break this session.

00:06:38.540 --> 00:06:42.770
Right now what you see depicted is a direct connection, end-to-end.

00:06:42.770 --> 00:06:47.576
I insert a proxy, I'm going to tell this guy, you're going to communicate directly with me

00:06:47.576 --> 00:06:51.589
and he is going to communicate directly with the back end Web server.

00:06:52.220 --> 00:06:53.449
Now we have two sessions.

00:06:53.450 --> 00:06:58.850
And what it is, is this guy thinks he's talking here, but in fact, he's talking to this guy.

00:06:59.630 --> 00:07:01.880
This guy thinks he's talking to this.

00:07:02.330 --> 00:07:04.279
In fact, he's talking to that.

00:07:04.760 --> 00:07:07.730
Now we have effectively a man-in-the-middle.

00:07:07.730 --> 00:07:09.200
But it's a good man in the middle.

00:07:09.200 --> 00:07:12.889
It's one that we put in there so that we can inspect the traffic.

00:07:13.130 --> 00:07:16.250
By the way, traffic coming in from the outside into my internal network.

00:07:16.520 --> 00:07:21.079
Maybe I want to have a look at that before I allow it in, see if it's got viruses or things like that.

00:07:21.440 --> 00:07:26.000
So I can inspect and I can enforce a security policy, if I need to.

00:07:26.330 --> 00:07:28.699
That's one of the advantages of putting in a proxy.

00:07:29.090 --> 00:07:36.559
By the way, sometimes people also put in proxies for privacy reasons, so that they can guard against who's seeing exactly who's doing what.

00:07:36.560 --> 00:07:42.649
It just looks like everything is coming from the proxy, not the actual end user, in that case.

00:07:43.580 --> 00:07:49.410
The final bit that I want to talk about here is actually maybe one of the most pervasive of these technologies,

00:07:49.410 --> 00:07:52.250
network address translation, or NAT for short.

00:07:52.760 --> 00:07:58.790
This is something that is probably in your home and you're probably using it right now and you may not know it.

00:07:59.330 --> 00:08:07.838
With NAT, what we do is, the Internet-- So by convention, we've all agreed by industry standard that there is a range of addresses

00:08:07.838 --> 00:08:13.549
that are reliable across the Internet and a range that are not reliable across the Internet.

00:08:13.730 --> 00:08:18.139
So this is specified in the in the rules for Internet traffic.

00:08:18.680 --> 00:08:23.125
If the address starts with a 10, then it doesn't matter what the other numbers are after that,

00:08:23.125 --> 00:08:30.529
because Internet addresses are always these four numbers separated by periods. That's the way we depict those.

00:08:30.890 --> 00:08:35.449
If it starts with a 10, if it's a 10 dot address, it is not routable across the Internet.

00:08:35.720 --> 00:08:40.789
It is routable across an internal intranet or across your home network.

00:08:40.940 --> 00:08:48.487
If you've got a home router, Wi-Fi router, whatever, you probably will find that it used a 10 dot address

00:08:48.487 --> 00:08:53.167
or more commonly it's used one of these 192.168 dot something dot something.

00:08:53.540 --> 00:08:55.489
That's very common in home setups.

00:08:56.060 --> 00:09:00.139
So this is an internal address that cannot be routed across the internet.

00:09:00.560 --> 00:09:02.330
That's why we need this NAT box.

00:09:02.720 --> 00:09:06.950
The NAT box does the translation, that's the T in NAT.

00:09:07.520 --> 00:09:14.944
So if this guy wants to send traffic that goes out to the Internet, his internal address, if he put that out directly, 

00:09:14.944 --> 00:09:17.809
it would hit the first router on the internet and get blocked.

00:09:17.810 --> 00:09:18.830
It wouldn't go anywhere.

00:09:19.370 --> 00:09:26.662
But in fact, what we're going to do, is the NAT table, the NAT router or NAT firewall maintains a table where it's going to translate

00:09:26.662 --> 00:09:34.100
this into an external address, usually just a single address that is recognized for everything that's behind here.

00:09:34.220 --> 00:09:36.080
So it actually conserves IP addresses.

00:09:36.080 --> 00:09:39.650
I don't need I could have 100 of these different devices back here.

00:09:40.010 --> 00:09:43.039
They all look like just one address going out to the Internet.

00:09:43.670 --> 00:09:49.018
So I'm going to translate the traffic as it comes here into something that's routable so it can get out there

00:09:49.018 --> 00:09:55.009
and it will come back and then the NAT box will turn it back and send it back to the workstation that it needed to go to.

00:09:55.580 --> 00:09:58.370
Now that's just to preserve existing functionality.

00:09:58.640 --> 00:09:59.037
Where's the protection?

00:09:59.037 --> 00:10:06.355
The protection comes in the fact that if this guy out here wants to directly hit this workstation,

00:10:06.355 --> 00:10:15.840
he can't because the address for this workstation, this 192.168.1.1 is example is not routable across the internet.

00:10:16.080 --> 00:10:18.510
If he tries to send that, it won't go anywhere.

00:10:19.020 --> 00:10:25.350
So this way we have internal traffic and external traffic and we can flow this way.

00:10:25.620 --> 00:10:30.119
But it prevent someone from being able to get from the outside directly to the inside.

00:10:30.330 --> 00:10:32.489
And as I said, this is very common technology.

00:10:32.730 --> 00:10:34.859
It's usually built into all of the home routers.

00:10:36.030 --> 00:10:38.489
Okay, we just talked about firewalls.

00:10:38.760 --> 00:10:40.919
Now we're going to talk about segmentation.

00:10:41.460 --> 00:10:48.270
That is, how are we going to apply these firewalls in various network architectures to achieve different levels of security?

00:10:48.840 --> 00:10:49.860
Let's take the first one.

00:10:49.890 --> 00:10:50.970
This is the most primitive.

00:10:51.180 --> 00:10:52.259
I don't recommend this.

00:10:52.380 --> 00:10:53.250
Don't ever do this.

00:10:53.250 --> 00:10:57.360
But this in the early days of the Internet was a viable option for a lot of people.

00:10:57.630 --> 00:10:58.740
It's a bastion host.

00:10:58.950 --> 00:11:06.000
We basically take our web server and put it on the Internet because what we don't want is the internal network--

00:11:06.000 --> 00:11:09.629
intranet --being exposed directly to the Internet.

00:11:10.080 --> 00:11:17.980
And if I don't put this somewhere outside here, then that means I have to allow all the Internet traffic into my internal network.

00:11:18.330 --> 00:11:19.679
And that's a really bad idea.

00:11:19.980 --> 00:11:23.578
So in the early days, people would put a single firewall right here,

00:11:23.578 --> 00:11:32.220
put their web server out here, or whatever devices, it had to be a bastion, last bastion on on the defense right out here on the edge.

00:11:32.820 --> 00:11:34.259
Again, not recommended.

00:11:34.770 --> 00:11:36.029
We have better ways to do it.

00:11:36.450 --> 00:11:42.413
The sort of next generation of this was a tri-homed network where in this case we've created

00:11:42.413 --> 00:11:48.149
essentially a firewall that we've carved off here into three different networks.

00:11:48.330 --> 00:11:53.909
So the firewall that's sitting here recognizes traffic coming in on one network interface card.

00:11:54.150 --> 00:12:01.440
And this traffic, for instance, all of the Internet traffic will be directed directly into where our Web server is.

00:12:01.650 --> 00:12:05.820
It's coming in on this network interface card, it will automatically be directed there.

00:12:05.940 --> 00:12:09.299
And maybe we apply some rules like the packet filtering and stuff like that.

00:12:09.300 --> 00:12:12.570
But that's where the traffic is destined to go in most cases.

00:12:13.050 --> 00:12:19.510
And likely in this case, the internal traffic, it's coming in on this network interface card,

00:12:19.510 --> 00:12:23.429
it could be routed here if somebody's internal user wants to hit our website.

00:12:23.640 --> 00:12:28.019
But it could also just as equally be trying to go out somewhere here.

00:12:28.320 --> 00:12:33.419
So here we have a single firewall sitting there, but it's got three different network interface cards.

00:12:33.420 --> 00:12:36.450
So it's trying to kind of do a lot of work in one place.

00:12:36.810 --> 00:12:38.850
And that's why it's called tri-homed.

00:12:38.850 --> 00:12:41.129
This is a DMZ of sorts.

00:12:41.490 --> 00:12:46.145
A demilitarized zone is the term we use there to refer to an area that's

00:12:46.145 --> 00:12:50.490
a buffer between an untrusted environment and a more trusted environment.

00:12:50.520 --> 00:12:54.905
Now, I'm going to use those terms very simplistically with apologies to people who understand what zero trust means,

00:12:54.905 --> 00:13:02.460
that there's really no trusted networks. But that's why we're essentially trying to show here's the red zone, the untrusted.

00:13:02.820 --> 00:13:07.950
The yellow zone is the semi trusted and the green zone is the more trusted zone.

00:13:08.190 --> 00:13:10.260
That's the idea here behind the color coding.

00:13:10.710 --> 00:13:13.650
Okay, move to the next one, the basic DMZ.

00:13:13.800 --> 00:13:15.275
And this is very popular.

00:13:15.690 --> 00:13:19.230
By the way, this last one is one that's often done in home networks.

00:13:19.500 --> 00:13:26.182
If you want to have an internal network and you want to allow guests to have access or your IoT devices or things like that.

00:13:26.250 --> 00:13:30.630
If you're hosting your own web server on your home network, not a great idea, but you could do it.

00:13:31.320 --> 00:13:34.620
You might want to separated out like this because again, it's a low cost option.

00:13:34.890 --> 00:13:37.529
That's the advantage is, it's very scalable.

00:13:37.770 --> 00:13:38.770
It's cheap.

00:13:39.240 --> 00:13:42.269
But on the downside, it's a single point of failure.

00:13:42.510 --> 00:13:47.159
If this thing doesn't do its job, everything's wide open potentially.

00:13:47.400 --> 00:13:52.229
Okay, moving on to the basic DMZ, which in this case I'm going to use two firewalls.

00:13:52.890 --> 00:13:59.570
So automatically I end up with more costs because I've got multiple security protections that I'm putting in place.

00:13:59.940 --> 00:14:05.250
And it's going to be more complex because I have to administer different rules and different capabilities here.

00:14:05.820 --> 00:14:08.500
And I gave an example of this in the first frame.

00:14:08.520 --> 00:14:15.310
In the first example before, when I talked about packet filtering and traffic coming in here and coming out there and so forth.

00:14:15.420 --> 00:14:17.429
That would be a basic DMZ.

00:14:18.030 --> 00:14:20.329
We got a red zone, a yellow zone, a green zone.

00:14:20.340 --> 00:14:21.690
Think of this as the traffic light.

00:14:22.020 --> 00:14:25.529
This is the danger untrusted, semi trusted caution.

00:14:25.680 --> 00:14:27.480
And then this is where it's more trusted.

00:14:27.750 --> 00:14:34.440
And we've built a kind of firewall rules to make sure that this can be trusted, because again, someone cannot go from here to here.

00:14:34.620 --> 00:14:35.620
We block that.

00:14:36.060 --> 00:14:39.990
We block that actually at this first firewall and then we have a secondary block here.

00:14:40.470 --> 00:14:47.040
And as a result, because we've got one block here and another block here, we have defense in depth.

00:14:47.580 --> 00:14:50.097
You remember going back to the very first video in this series,

00:14:50.097 --> 00:14:54.400
one of the principles I talked about that was important is this notion of defense in depth.

00:14:54.480 --> 00:14:57.630
I don't rely on any single security mechanism to protect me.

00:14:57.960 --> 00:15:04.130
If this firewall fails for some reason, I still can't get traffic from here to there because I've built in a rule

00:15:04.130 --> 00:15:09.869
that said the source address has to be traffic coming from this web server, for instance.

00:15:10.140 --> 00:15:16.380
And if this failed and all the traffic was able to come through it, then it would still be blocked by this second.

00:15:16.620 --> 00:15:17.879
So we've got defense in depth.

00:15:18.210 --> 00:15:19.320
It's also more scalable.

00:15:19.740 --> 00:15:24.659
So I could build up multiples of these, multiples of a lot of these kinds of things.

00:15:24.660 --> 00:15:27.140
So the opportunities are a lot greater.

00:15:27.150 --> 00:15:30.269
Again, not a single point of failure, but defense in depth.

00:15:31.080 --> 00:15:34.020
And then finally, I'll talk about a multi-tiered DMZ.

00:15:34.350 --> 00:15:39.169
So the multi-tiered DMZ, we basically put a firewall here and here.

00:15:39.320 --> 00:15:43.220
So now we've got this this diagram essentially replicated here.

00:15:43.580 --> 00:15:49.606
But in this case, I've split out the web server from the application server from the database, in this example.

00:15:50.630 --> 00:15:55.880
In this case, I'm going to implement yet another firewall, a third firewall in this case.

00:15:55.970 --> 00:16:01.429
So as you would guess, one of the downsides is it's going to be even more expensive than these others.

00:16:01.730 --> 00:16:05.512
It is going to be more costly and complex than these others,

00:16:05.512 --> 00:16:09.440
because now I've got three firewalls to administer and different rules on each one of them.

00:16:09.890 --> 00:16:14.090
However, we've got defense in depth on steroids.

00:16:14.180 --> 00:16:20.469
We've got even more because now any one of these mechanisms, it would have to be that all three of them failed.

00:16:20.469 --> 00:16:24.470
If one of them failed, it wouldn't necessarily be a huge problem for us.

00:16:24.860 --> 00:16:26.659
We also have greater granularity.

00:16:26.990 --> 00:16:31.039
That is, I can allow traffic to only go to here from this zone to this zone.

00:16:31.310 --> 00:16:39.349
I can allow traffic from this zone only to go to that zone and traffic from this zone only to go to that zone and do the reverse back.

00:16:39.590 --> 00:16:43.759
So more granularity, more firewalls, more cost and complexity.

00:16:43.940 --> 00:16:46.399
But potentially, if I do it right, more security.

00:16:47.430 --> 00:16:51.989
Okay, in the previous section, we talked about firewalls and segmentation.

00:16:52.140 --> 00:16:56.610
Now we're going to cover the next subject of virtual private networking VPNs.

00:16:57.000 --> 00:16:58.950
Now, what are VPNs designed to do?

00:16:59.220 --> 00:17:03.990
They're basically trying to give us a secure channel over an untrusted network.

00:17:04.230 --> 00:17:05.279
That's the idea.

00:17:05.520 --> 00:17:12.419
I can't necessarily trust the Internet because I don't control I don't have visibility into all of the aspects of that.

00:17:12.810 --> 00:17:17.700
But I'd like to be able to send secure information or information in a secure way over it.

00:17:18.030 --> 00:17:20.880
So I want a secure channel over an untrusted network.

00:17:21.150 --> 00:17:22.499
That would be a great capability.

00:17:23.130 --> 00:17:28.769
And the way I do that, I accomplish it by encrypting my information and then sending it over the network.

00:17:29.070 --> 00:17:31.495
The idea there is I get confidentiality.

00:17:31.800 --> 00:17:35.730
And I get that because people can't see what is in the packet.

00:17:36.000 --> 00:17:37.739
All they'll see is the encrypted information.

00:17:38.220 --> 00:17:41.399
We lots of times think about this as a pipe or as a tunnel.

00:17:41.430 --> 00:17:43.770
You'll hear those kind of analogies used here.

00:17:44.070 --> 00:17:51.933
Think about if we've got a user here with a browser trying to get to a web server and we're building a secure pipe,

00:17:51.933 --> 00:17:54.269
a connection from one end to the other.

00:17:54.510 --> 00:17:57.089
And I'm encrypting all the packets as they go across.

00:17:57.390 --> 00:18:03.209
So that way someone who looks here sees nothing that they can interpret, nothing that means anything.

00:18:03.450 --> 00:18:07.710
That's the idea behind a secure pipe, secure channel over an untrusted network.

00:18:08.040 --> 00:18:09.480
So that's the good stuff.

00:18:09.480 --> 00:18:12.450
And security guys love that we can do that.

00:18:12.780 --> 00:18:14.670
What they don't love is this.

00:18:15.300 --> 00:18:24.869
That is a limited inspection capability, this ability to-- so the good guy can send their traffic without everybody seeing it.

00:18:25.180 --> 00:18:30.540
It also means a bad guy could send their traffic without everybody seeing it and that would be a problem.

00:18:30.660 --> 00:18:37.170
So it limits our ability to inspect and therefore see if someone's putting malware into my system or someone's initiating an attack.

00:18:37.530 --> 00:18:40.470
So it's one of those blessings and a curse at the same time.

00:18:41.160 --> 00:18:44.609
All right. There are different kinds of VPN technologies.

00:18:44.820 --> 00:18:51.240
And to understand them, we really need a little bit of understanding on network technologies.

00:18:51.600 --> 00:18:53.339
A 7 layer OSI stack.

00:18:53.370 --> 00:18:54.390
This is classic stuff.

00:18:54.400 --> 00:18:55.799
We're not going to go into it in detail.

00:18:55.830 --> 00:18:58.357
But the notion is, is that there are different layers.

00:18:58.410 --> 00:19:03.959
For every packet I send, different concerns, different aspects that are implemented at different layers here.

00:19:04.380 --> 00:19:08.338
And what happens in the real world is most people, if you're an application programmer,

00:19:08.338 --> 00:19:12.930
you're really concerned more up here with the application, presentation layers, these kinds of things.

00:19:13.500 --> 00:19:17.262
And the networking infrastructure, people are much more concerned with the stuff down here

00:19:17.262 --> 00:19:21.239
at the transport network data link, physical layers and those kinds of things.

00:19:21.630 --> 00:19:25.170
So there's a little bit off concerns that separate there.

00:19:25.560 --> 00:19:32.530
But the other thing that's really important about this is, with this model, we have a way of, if you implement a security capability,

00:19:32.530 --> 00:19:37.710
for instance, at one of these layers, it's inherited by the upper layers.

00:19:37.980 --> 00:19:43.019
So if I encrypt all my traffic here, then it's encrypted by all the higher layers as well.

00:19:43.380 --> 00:19:49.170
So from a simple security standpoint, it might be easier to put the encryption down lower in the stack.

00:19:49.440 --> 00:19:52.860
We'll talk in a minute what hat's not necessarily what you always want to do, though.

00:19:53.460 --> 00:19:56.289
So there are different examples of how we do this.

00:19:57.120 --> 00:19:59.009
For instance, at the application layer.

00:19:59.280 --> 00:20:01.770
You may have heard of this protocol--secure shell.

00:20:02.010 --> 00:20:07.320
That's an example of an application layer or application specific VPN.

00:20:07.470 --> 00:20:12.509
It encrypts the data so that you can connect into a particular device console, this sort of thing.

00:20:12.750 --> 00:20:15.869
There's a secure FTP and other examples like that.

00:20:16.560 --> 00:20:22.227
Another one that's very common and you're going to run across this all the time, whether you're aware of it or not,

00:20:22.227 --> 00:20:28.619
is TLS or SSL--transport layer security or secure sockets layer.

00:20:29.100 --> 00:20:30.450
This is the older term.

00:20:30.450 --> 00:20:32.819
The newer name for this standard is TLS.

00:20:33.120 --> 00:20:34.949
It's implemented at the transport layer.

00:20:35.220 --> 00:20:42.480
That's what you usually see when you're a browser connected to a web server and you see that little lock in the browser up on the the URL line.

00:20:42.870 --> 00:20:45.209
That's what is is being implemented there, TLS.

00:20:46.350 --> 00:20:50.489
So that is everything that's going to that Web server then is going to be encrypted.

00:20:51.240 --> 00:20:52.589
There are other examples.

00:20:53.010 --> 00:20:57.269
There's a thing called IPsec, which is implemented at the network layer.

00:20:57.630 --> 00:21:02.847
If you do that, then everything between two network addresses will be encrypted

00:21:02.847 --> 00:21:06.840
as opposed to between the web server or for a very specific application.

00:21:07.320 --> 00:21:16.289
And then we have some other examples of point-to-point tunneling protocol, P2PTP or L2TP, which is the layer 2 tunneling protocol.

00:21:16.590 --> 00:21:18.779
These are some examples of even lower on the stack.

00:21:18.990 --> 00:21:24.990
So not to go into details on it, but just to give you an idea, there's not a single type of VPN or VPN technology.

00:21:25.290 --> 00:21:27.089
They all share some of these qualities.

00:21:27.270 --> 00:21:31.769
And if you see these things, you should think, ah that's a type of VPN.

00:21:32.430 --> 00:21:37.972
Now what's happening these days, is we're tending to move away from these broad network based VPNs

00:21:37.972 --> 00:21:41.579
more toward application specific VPNs.

00:21:41.880 --> 00:21:43.049
What's the reason for that?

00:21:43.290 --> 00:21:47.970
Well, on the advantage side for the broad ones, is that they're relatively simple.

00:21:48.180 --> 00:21:53.118
I set up a connection, for instance, between two endpoints or me into a particular network.

00:21:53.610 --> 00:22:01.109
And everything I do, for instance, if I set up an IPsec session, everything I send into that whole network now will be encrypted.

00:22:01.350 --> 00:22:03.930
So that's a very simple type of thing to do.

00:22:04.560 --> 00:22:11.412
However, it doesn't give us the granularity that we get over on this side with a very application specific firewall

00:22:11.412 --> 00:22:14.107
or VPN like we're doing here with SSH.

00:22:15.210 --> 00:22:19.609
The other advantage on the broad based side, network-based side, is a catch all.

00:22:19.980 --> 00:22:27.569
Again, if I encrypt at this layer, then all the traffic, all the different applications can benefit from that basic VPN.

00:22:27.600 --> 00:22:28.980
I don't have to set up separate ones.

00:22:29.250 --> 00:22:31.109
So it's simpler in that regard.

00:22:31.470 --> 00:22:34.811
However, I don't have as much control and as much granularity.

00:22:35.490 --> 00:22:41.324
The ability to control and say I have a VPN for my email, a VPN for my file sharing application,

00:22:41.324 --> 00:22:48.000
a VPN for my instant messaging application and control all of those separately gives me more control

00:22:48.000 --> 00:22:53.849
so that if I need to shut a particular service down or a particular user down, I can do that.

00:22:54.210 --> 00:22:57.839
So a lot of different possibilities here in this area of VPNs.

00:22:58.680 --> 00:23:04.055
Okay, now we've covered firewalls, segmentation, VPNs, and our last one is SASE.

00:23:05.460 --> 00:23:06.210
What is SASE?.

00:23:06.210 --> 00:23:09.809
It's secure access service edge.

00:23:09.930 --> 00:23:11.459
That's what the acronym stands for.

00:23:11.610 --> 00:23:18.359
It's actually a very relevant and important topic these days as part of the larger subject of zero trust.

00:23:18.690 --> 00:23:22.435
And in fact, another aspect of zero trust that's very relevant is micro-segmentation.

00:23:23.250 --> 00:23:27.610
And we talked about segmentation before, but micro-segmentation just carries that to the extreme

00:23:27.610 --> 00:23:33.023
and puts lots and lots of zones within your network with little micro networks.

00:23:33.870 --> 00:23:35.279
But SASE in particular.

00:23:35.280 --> 00:23:42.510
What we're trying to do here is create some sort of of secure capability that's delivered on the edge.

00:23:42.930 --> 00:23:44.130
To think about it this way.

00:23:44.250 --> 00:23:45.520
Let's look at a Venn diagram.

00:23:46.770 --> 00:23:48.900
Here, we've got networking concerns here.

00:23:49.320 --> 00:23:52.020
We've got security things and we've got the cloud.

00:23:52.380 --> 00:23:59.174
If you think about the intersection of network security and cloud, this is the world where SASE lives.

00:23:59.255 --> 00:24:03.583
Because-- and depending on how you want to think about this, if you think more mathematically,

00:24:03.583 --> 00:24:07.290
you might like this description, if you think more visually, you might like this one.

00:24:07.500 --> 00:24:09.180
We'll go through the mathematical one first.

00:24:09.930 --> 00:24:16.049
SASE is basically network security plus WAN, wide area networking capability.

00:24:16.050 --> 00:24:20.700
So that's the network and the security all delivered from the cloud.

00:24:20.700 --> 00:24:28.290
So that's the way of taking that Venn diagram and expressing it kind of as a loose mathematical equation.

00:24:28.290 --> 00:24:33.979
So if we were to decompose this a little more, what does it mean? What does the NetSEC mean, network security?

00:24:33.979 --> 00:24:36.670
Well, it's basically firewalling, which we've talked about.

00:24:36.670 --> 00:24:44.738
It's secure web gateways, which we didn't really go into detail, but think about those as application-specific firewalls and things of that sort.

00:24:45.482 --> 00:24:52.385
And DLP, data loss prevention, which is something I'll talk about in the data security domain when we get to that topic.

00:24:52.385 --> 00:24:59.300
But all of these things and more, delivered on the edge, so that's the network security component of all this.

00:24:59.300 --> 00:25:05.780
The WAN, specifically, is a software-defined WAN. Which is a way of creating a dynamic network

00:25:05.780 --> 00:25:11.830
where you can change where the boundaries of the network are and provision these in real-time, effectively.

00:25:11.830 --> 00:25:14.169
So it gives you a lot more agility and flexibility.

00:25:14.350 --> 00:25:21.133
So we're adding that capability, this SD-WAN marrying it, merging it, with the network security components

00:25:21.133 --> 00:25:26.260
and then delivering the thing from the cloud, because the cloud gives us the ability to do scalability.

00:25:26.440 --> 00:25:29.934
We can scale up and scale down elasticity and agility.

00:25:30.310 --> 00:25:32.409
Again, lots and lots of flexibility.

00:25:32.590 --> 00:25:34.539
That's what people are looking for in this case.

00:25:34.870 --> 00:25:39.784
If I take all of those things and then maybe even add in another thing within the security space,

00:25:39.784 --> 00:25:44.046
identity management, specifically, authentication and authorization.

00:25:44.200 --> 00:25:48.130
So some access controls, then this is what SASE is about.

00:25:48.430 --> 00:25:56.542
It's combining all of these functions into a single logical component and delivering that from the cloud, at the edge of the network.

00:25:56.990 --> 00:25:58.579
Another way to look at it is this way.

00:25:58.790 --> 00:26:06.619
So here we've got our users, here we've got the external network, and then I've got this SASE capability that's here in the middle.

00:26:07.250 --> 00:26:12.566
And what this delivers is on one end, the networking capabilities I mentioned previously,

00:26:12.566 --> 00:26:18.979
on the other end, also the security capabilities-- firewall and DLP and so forth.

00:26:19.250 --> 00:26:23.960
So all of this is is a way of combining these functions and delivering them.

00:26:24.200 --> 00:26:29.430
This is a more modern way of delivering all of these capabilities as opposed to what would have in the past

00:26:29.430 --> 00:26:35.081
each one of these would have been a separate appliance, a separate component, a separate administrative capability,

00:26:35.081 --> 00:26:39.079
a separate person to administer it and all of those kinds of things.

00:26:39.320 --> 00:26:43.819
So it's bringing these functions together and having them operate in a more holistic way.

00:26:44.630 --> 00:26:47.030
All right, now, we finished the networking topic.

00:26:47.210 --> 00:26:50.059
A couple of things that I didn't cover and I put here in the etc.

00:26:50.180 --> 00:26:54.084
just because of the interest of time, I didn't really get into very much in the physical networking side,

00:26:54.084 --> 00:26:59.420
things like 5G and Wi-Fi and the network security capabilities of those.

00:26:59.750 --> 00:27:04.520
If there's interest in that, put that down in the comments and maybe we'll revisit that in a future video.

00:27:05.240 --> 00:27:10.160
Until then, now we've completed the networking portion of our domains.

00:27:10.490 --> 00:27:14.180
The next one and, we want you to stay tuned and look for that one.

00:27:14.300 --> 00:27:18.829
The next one will be in the area of application security as we move along the various domains.

00:27:19.980 --> 00:27:26.939
Please remember to like, subscribe and hit notify so that you'll be aware when future videos in the series are available.

